[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWT-29
TITLE: Time Keeping
STATEMENT: >>>
To perform the time counting, the firmware utilizes one of the MCU's internal timer hardware
peripherals. The hardware timer peripheral was selected as it can run independently from the CPU
instructions and keep accurate time, while not having to deal with other processing such as
handling messaging to and from the main control MCU. One downside to this is that the time value
must be manually copied and updated every time it needs to be sent to the main controller. In
firmware this is done every time the main controller requests a new value via the I2C
communication. The tradeoff is worth it, as the small amount of time spent copying is much less
than the extra processing timer required if the MCU attempted to constantly 'manually' update a
value at the same rate.

The timer peripheral is configured to utilize an external 32.768 kHz crystal as a clock source for
increased accuracy. No pre-scaler values are used, and so the count rate of the timer is 32.768
kHz. The 'raw' time value is represented by 'ticks', where 32,768 'ticks' represent one second of
time passage. The firmware utilizes the desired operational timing value in 'ticks' as the value for
triggering the hardware peripheral's comparison interrupt. The hardware peripheral utilizes 32-bit
registers for counting and for comparison, so the maximum timer runtime is 36 hours.

The method by which the emission timeout period is determined is by using the peripheral
hardware's compare functionality. After being reset and then enabled, the timer peripheral will start
counting up from 0. By setting the desired timeout tick period in the peripheral's compare register,
an interrupt service routine is triggered once the count value of the timer's value register matches
the stored compare register value. The firmware can then stop emission output within the interrupt
processing function that is called.
<<<
